Windows: Run ays-brut.cmd; it unpacks itself and opens your default browser
Other platforms: Open ays-brut.uncompressed.html in your web browser
Requires: Chrome/Edge 113+, Firefox 141+ or Safari 26+
Tested on: Chrome 152, Edge 156 and Firefox 157

Renders at 1600x900, NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4070 or higher recommended.

Source code: https://github.com/BartmanAbyss/ays-brut


Credits and references:

    Prior art and packaging:

    - INCENDIUM (Bercon + pestis, Revision 2025) - 4 kb WebGPU intro with a 3D
      fluid sim and compute-shader music. The packaging model, the byte budget
      and the dropper's trick - PowerShell serving its own bytes as
      "Content-Encoding: br" - come from it. MIT.
    - Roquefort (Bercon) - 256^3 WebGPU fluid. MIT.
    - Shadertoy work by wyatt and michael0884 informed the
      nearest-particle-as-SDF idea. Studied only - Shadertoy defaults to
      CC BY-NC-SA.
    - Pakettic (Veikko Sariola / pestis, MIT) - the model for build/pack.py:
      packing as a search over orderings and names, scored by compressed size,
      with late-acceptance hill climbing. Reimplemented.
    - Shader Minifier (Laurent Le Brun / Ctrl-Alt-Test, Apache-2.0) - the
      trick of letting a local take the name of a global its function never
      touches. Idea only.

    Rendering:

    - Sphere tracing - J. C. Hart, "Sphere tracing: a geometric method for the
      antialiased ray tracing of implicit surfaces", The Visual Computer
      12(10), 1996.
    - Over-relaxed sphere tracing - B. Keinert, H. SchÑfer, J. Korndîrfer,
      U. Ganse, M. Stamminger, "Enhanced Sphere Tracing", STAG 2014
      (Eurographics Italian Chapter), doi:10.2312/stag.20141233.
    - Distance-field modelling - Inigo Quilez's articles: SDF primitives
      (capped cylinder, capsule) and the polynomial smooth minimum used for
      the glass's joins (iquilezles.org/articles/distfunctions, /smin).
    - Nested dielectrics by priority (glass, champagne and bottle glass
      meeting without coincident surfaces) - C. M. Schmidt, B. Budge, "Simple
      Nested Dielectrics in Ray Traced Images", Journal of Graphics Tools
      7(2):1-8, 2002.
    - Fresnel term - C. Schlick, "An Inexpensive BRDF Model for
      Physically-based Rendering", Computer Graphics Forum 13(3), 1994
      (Schlick's approximation).
    - Absorption - the Beer-Lambert law.
    - Caustics by tracing photons from the light - after H. W. Jensen,
      "Global Illumination using Photon Maps", Eurographics Rendering
      Workshop 1996; here splatted into a table texture.
    - Jump flooding (the spray's far field) - G. Rong, T.-S. Tan, "Jump
      Flooding in GPU with Applications to Voronoi Diagram and Distance
      Transform", I3D 2006.
    - Hash - pcg3d from M. Jarzynski, M. Olano, "Hash Functions for GPU
      Rendering", Journal of Computer Graphics Techniques 9(3):21-38, 2020.
    - Tonemapping - K. Narkowicz's fit of the ACES filmic curve, "ACES Filmic
      Tone Mapping Curve" (blog, 2016).
    - Smootherstep (shapes and camera easing) - the quintic fade from
      K. Perlin, "Improving Noise", SIGGRAPH 2002.

    Simulation:

    - Collision as a position constraint (no tunnelling through the 1.4 mm
      glass at any speed) - in the spirit of M. MÅller, B. Heidelberger,
      M. Hennix, J. Ratcliff, "Position Based Dynamics", J. Visual
      Communication and Image Representation 18(2), 2007.
    - The per-glass surface waves are the discrete 2D wave equation.
      Textbook, no single source.

    Sound:

    - Bubble acoustics - M. Minnaert, "On musical air-bubbles and the sounds
      of running water", Philosophical Magazine 16(104):235-248, 1933 (the
      resonance 3/r).
    - The liquid's bubble model (damping, pitch rise, size and depth
      distributions) - K. van den Doel, "Physically Based Models for Liquid
      Sounds", ACM Transactions on Applied Perception 2(4), 2005; first at
      ICAD 2004.
    - Fizz (bubbles bursting at the surface, pitch rising as the cavity
      collapses) - M. Poujol, R. Wunenburger, F. Ollivier, A. Antkowiak,
      J. Pierre, "Sound of effervescence", Physical Review Fluids 6, 013604,
      2021.
    - The cork pop - shaped after G. Liger-Belair, D. Cordier, R. Georges,
      "Under-expanded supersonic CO2 freezing jets during champagne cork
      popping", Science Advances 5(9), 2019, doi:10.1126/sciadv.aav5528; and
      the Helmholtz resonance of the open neck.
    - The pad - PADsynth, by Nasca Octavian Paul (ZynAddSubFX): harmonics as
      bands of frequencies with random phases in one looped table. A lite
      version without the FFT.
    - Champagne acoustics background (not implemented directly): K. Spratt,
      K. Lee, P. Wilson, "Champagne acoustics", Physics Today 2018.

    Build tools:

    - wgslender (Hugo Daniel, CC0-1.0) - WGSL tree-shaking.
    - terser (BSD-2-Clause) - JS minification, with its dependencies acorn,
      commander, source-map-support, buffer-from, @jridgewell/* (MIT) and
      source-map (BSD-3-Clause).
    - Closure Compiler (Google, Apache-2.0), v20261015 native Windows build -
      JS optimisation in SIMPLE mode, before esbuild and terser.
    - esbuild (Evan Wallace, MIT), 0.28.2 native Windows build - between
      Closure and terser.
    - Brotli (Google, MIT) - via Python's brotli package.
    - Late-acceptance hill climbing (the packer search) - E. K. Burke,
      Y. Bykov, "The Late Acceptance Hill-Climbing Heuristic", European
      Journal of Operational Research 258(1), 2017 (Bykov's 2008 report
      first).
    - Roadroller (Kang Seonghoon, MIT), in Aske Simon Christensen's
      (Blueberry's) intro-size fork, https://github.com/askeksa/roadroller -
      packs the page's script. Its decoder, which ships, is public domain.

    Font:

    - Josefin Sans (Santiago Orozco, SIL Open Font License 1.1) - the
      lettering's capitals, redrawn as pen strokes.

